{"authored" [{"question" "q-3762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-3762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-2+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-3762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8fc4a62db3ddd630a473cdc2c920f268b90c9706f019f17762a4e08b4154258" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/1396495