le" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-3762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "Does not exist."] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8fc4a62db3ddd630a473cdc2c920f268b90c9706f019f17762a4e08b4154258" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{5-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8fc4a62db3ddd630a473cdc2c920f268b90c9706f019f17762a4e08b4154258" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339623" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{2+0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8fc4a62db3ddd630a473cdc2c920f268b90c9706f019f17762a4e08b4154258" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptote"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-7{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-7{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{2}+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8fc4a62db3ddd630a473cdc2c920f268b90c9706f019f17762a4e08b4154258" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b6837