} {"question" "q-78008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7c8f54aab65f3c5f9257b726f179ce8dbf0d4e6fb61eaf0f00c6594b4d579d3" "basis" 857 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7c8f54aab65f3c5f9257b726f179ce8dbf0d4e6fb61eaf0f00c6594b4d579d3" "basis" 857 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e6efbab36c4cc15ca5a9a199e2d7fe3b8275fcf97866a9e0075dd16a2e1a6af" "basis" 857 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course