, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{5-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-339623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-339623" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-339623" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\infty "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\infty "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-339612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-48717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x<0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|3x+6|}{x-4}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-48717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|3x+6|=-(3x+6).$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|3x+6|}{x-4}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x<0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3+\\frac{6}{x})}{(1-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(3+0)}{1-0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-48717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-48717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-48717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-3"] ["math" "y=-6"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=3"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-48717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8}$ is $y=\\frac{1}{4},$ find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{32x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(5-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(c+\\frac{32}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-0}{c+0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{c} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ c & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-35829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-302242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}}$ is $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ find the value of $c.$\n\n$c=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-302242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{18{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(18+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(c-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{18+0}{c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{c} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 36 & =3c \\\\ c & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-302242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-302242" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-302242" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{2+0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=1"] ["math" "y=2"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{24{x}^{3}-1}{2c{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}}$ is $y=-\\frac{1}{3},$ find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{24{x}^{3}-1}{2c{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{24{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(24-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(2c-\\frac{15}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-0}{2c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ c & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-35836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-36"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-35836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-36"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-101319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-101319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-0)}{(3+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-101319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-101319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-101319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{3}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-101319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptote"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{11{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(11-9x)}{(2-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11-9x}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(11-9x) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-106702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "y=2"] ["math" "y=\\frac{11}{2}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptote"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}}{6-2x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}}{6-2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{5}}{x}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{2x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7{x}^{4}-3x)}{(\\frac{6}{x}-2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7{x}^{4}-3x)}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(7{x}^{4}-3x) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-106698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-3"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=2"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{3}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-106698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptote"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-7{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-7{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{2}+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-68072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptote"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{4{x}^{3}-x+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{4{x}^{3}-x+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}+0)}{(4-0+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "676328569b5bb4acd4e792fd6c7af932391b625915f768e9cf989243045eaf29"} {"question" "q-78016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{5}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "q-78016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptote"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ddf5288019cfd5f3c4cb4ed49b8844a48d3681d6b3bf5237b26060e29f8e3c6"} {"question" "e-80" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-80.json" "file_sha256" "60c5ed6a766c656a303b73c5804deda1b6ed503e713cfef053486c1ced6c746e"} {"question" "e-80" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{4}+9x}{6{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{9x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-80.json" "file_sha256" "60c5ed6a766c656a303b73c5804deda1b6ed503e713cfef053486c1ced6c746e"} {"question" "e-81" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-81.json" "file_sha256" "87bf075eda97a8a3fe1b6ad205cb07cbe4225ba7055fcda334e4b4499c131740"} {"question" "e-81" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4-5x-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-2x-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-2x+0+0}{1+0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-81.json" "file_sha256" "87bf075eda97a8a3fe1b6ad205cb07cbe4225ba7055fcda334e4b4499c131740"} {"question" "e-82" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^3+1}{x^3-15x}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-82.json" "file_sha256" "0192f58ca228592b65fe2aefd67c59c3311a0927511ccc127e1aabeced00de0f"} {"question" "e-82" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{3}-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{15}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-82.json" "file_sha256" "0192f58ca228592b65fe2aefd67c59c3311a0927511ccc127e1aabeced00de0f"} {"question" "e-83" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-83.json" "file_sha256" "b1d65fc1fb7c2d9772ec79ce33675d629d5803300cc76b17637dbefa6be70ecb"} {"question" "e-83" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}-10}{{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{1-0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-83.json" "file_sha256" "b1d65fc1fb7c2d9772ec79ce33675d629d5803300cc76b17637dbefa6be70ecb"}] "usage" {"q-35763" [] "e-83" [] "q-68072" [] "q-3762" [] "e-82" [] "q-106702" [] "q-3761" [] "q-68048" [] "q-339612" [] "q-339623" [] "e-81" [] "e-80" [] "q-78008" []} "basis" 1958 "retractions" [[17592186125005 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{x^{4} - 6}{x^{2} - x + 2}$."] [17592186124827 :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\frac{2x + 5}{2x^{3} + 2x + 6}$."] [17592186124310 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}$."] [17592186124310 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4$. Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{3x^{4} + 9x}{6x^{4} - x^{3}} &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{3x^{4}}{x^{4}} + \\frac{9x}{x^{4}})}{(\\frac{6x^{4}}{x^{4}} - \\frac{x^{3}}{x^{4}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(3 + \\frac{9}{x^{3}})}{(6 - \\frac{1}{x})} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 0}{6 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124312 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}$."] [17592186124312 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2$. Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{4 - 5x - 2x^{3}}{x^{2} + 3} &= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{-2x^{3}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{5x}{x^{2}} + \\frac{4}{x^{2}})}{(\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} + \\frac{3}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{(-2x - \\frac{5}{x} + \\frac{4}{x^{2}})}{(1 + \\frac{3}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}(\\frac{-2x + 0 + 0}{1 + 0}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}(-2x) \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124313 :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^3+1}{x^3-15x}$?"] [17592186124313 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{2x^{3} + 1}{x^{3} - 15x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})}{(\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{15x}{x^{3}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(2 + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})}{(1 - \\frac{15}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 + 0}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2$."] [17592186124326 :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}$."] [17592186124326 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{x^{5} - 10}{x^{3} - 8x^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{x^{5}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}})}{(\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{8x^{2}}{x^{3}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(x^{2} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}})}{(1 - \\frac{8}{x})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}(\\frac{x^{2} - 0}{1 - 0}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}x^{2} \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186121985 17592186121986 17592186121987 17592186121988 17592186121989 17592186123053 17592186123054 17592186123055 17592186123056 17592186123057 17592186275157 17592186275158 17592186275159 17592186275160 17592186275161 17592186275170 17592186275176 17592186275177 17592186275201 17592186275202 17592186275203 17592186275204 17592186